' Listing 3 of Grogono's article (BYTE, July 1979, p. 208): a song, sung
' by a recursive macro.
' Transcribed from the scan: the only changes are ^ for the printed up
' arrow, straight quotes, spacing and line breaks outside strings, and these
' comment lines. Strings are as printed (spaces inside strings are read
' from proportional type, so their exact count is approximate).
' As printed, W has no leading space, so the song prints
' "GREEN BOTTLESSTANDING ON THE WALL". That is presumably not what was
' meant (an interpretation, not a fault in the program: it runs exactly as
' written). "$W " STANDING ON THE WALL" @" gives the intended words.
' The prompt is followed by no new line, so the first verse starts on the
' prompt's line.
( "HOW MANY VERSES?"
  S ? = S. ^ 10 S. - [ #V,S. 1 +; ] )

$V %A 1 - [ #N,%A; #B; #W; ",!"
            #N,%A; #B; ";!" #F; ",!"
            #N,%A 1 -; #B; #W; ".!!"
            #V,%A 1 -; ] @

$B " GREEN BOTTLES" @
$W "STANDING ON THE WALL" @
$F "IF ONE OF THOSE BOTTLES SHOULD HAPPEN TO FALL" @

$N %A 9 - [ "NINE"  @ ]
   %A 8 - [ "EIGHT" @ ]
   %A 7 - [ "SEVEN" @ ]
   %A 6 - [ "SIX"   @ ]
   %A 5 - [ "FIVE"  @ ]
   %A 4 - [ "FOUR"  @ ]
   %A 3 - [ "THREE" @ ]
   %A 2 - [ "TWO"   @ ]
   %A 1 - [ "ONE"   @ ]
   %A     [ "NO"    @ ] @
$$
